{"id":"nasa-115ew51","src":"nasa","t":"APOD: 2026 November 12 – Moon, Venus and Spica Meet","u":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-12-moon-venus-and-spica-meet/","at":"2026-11-12T05:05:00.000Z","by":"","img":null,"body":[{"p":["APOD APOD"]},{"p":["Astronomy Picture of the Day"]},{"p":["Discover the cosmos! Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer."]},{"img":{"src":"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/conjunction-bubak.jpg?w=1638&h=2048&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint","cap":"A crescent moon and bright Venus appear beyond a mountain in a rural area at dusk."}},{"h":"Moon, Venus and Spica Meet"},{"p":[{"b":"Explanation:"}," ",{"a":"https://tolkiengateway.net/wiki/Riddle_of_Strider","t":"Not all those who wander are lost."}," A night sky example is that ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/","t":"planets"}," wander, and in fact the very word “planet” comes from “wandering star” in Greek. As planets ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-may-30-mars-in-the-loop/","t":"shift"}," relative to ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/planetary-motion/","t":"fixed stars"},", they may appear from our perspective to ",{"a":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504595403659-9088ce801e29","t":"get closer"}," to other astronomical objects. Such ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-march-4-10-days-of-venus-and-jupiter/","t":"meetings"}," are called ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2020-december-15-great-conjunction-saturn-and-jupiter-converge/","t":"conjunctions"},". They don’t happen by chance, but follow the precise laws of ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/orbits-and-keplers-laws/","t":"celestial mechanics"},". This peaceful ",{"a":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DfciOD3DKOE/?img_index=1","t":"image"},", captured in ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poland","t":"Poland"},", shows the ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-april-24-young-moon-and-sister-stars/","t":"crescent Moon"}," above the ",{"a":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x1YiJgA8lpw","t":"Tatra Mountains"},", and bright ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/venus/","t":"Venus"}," close by, a little lower in the horizon. The bright star to the left of Venus is ",{"a":"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/news/understanding-spica","t":"Spica"}," in the constellation of ",{"a":"https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/virgo/","t":"Virgo"},", the Maiden. Spica is ",{"a":"https://earthsky.org/brightest-stars/speed-on-to-spica-the-15th-brightest-star/","t":"one of the brightest stars"}," in the sky, only about 250 light-years away. It was found in 1890 that ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spica","t":"Spica"}," is a binary star: two massive blue stars orbiting each other every 4 days. But earlier this year it was reported that Spica, the current companion of wandering Venus, is actually a ",{"a":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2515-5172/aea83b","t":"triple system"},"."]},{"p":[{"b":"Tomorrow’s picture:"}," what’s next?"]},{"more":true},{"p":[{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/random-apod/","t":"Random APOD Generator"}]},{"p":[{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-11-ngc-4449-small-galaxy-big-impact/","t":"Yesterday’s Image APOD: 2026 November 11 – NGC 4449: Small Galaxy, Big Impact"},"Tomorrow’s Image"]}]}
